    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. You mentioned keeping it clear of bugs though—how much of a problem is a blockage actually? Like, if something gets inside the tube, how does that mess with the reading? Is it just a minor error or can it totally fail the instrument?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A blockage is a serious problem—it can completely fail the instrument, not just cause a minor error. If the pitot tube’s opening is blocked (say, by a bug or ice), the ram air can’t enter, so the dynamic pressure stays at whatever it was when the blockage occurred. That means the airspeed indicator will freeze at that value, even if you speed up or slow down. Worse, if the drain hole gets blocked too, the trapped air can expand or contract with altitude changes, causing the needle to swing wildly—sometimes showing zero or even pegging past redline. In a Cessna, that’s a classic recipe for a stall or overspeed without warning, which is why preflight checks always include a pitot cover removal and a visual inspection.",
      "source": "target",
