      "role": "user",
      "content": "so it can just totally lie to you? that sounds terrifying. are there any specific types of obstructions that pilots have to worry about most besides just ice? like, is there anything else that tends to crawl in there or mess with the pressure?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Oh, absolutely—it can lie to you with a straight face, and that’s why pilots treat it like a live grenade. Beyond ice, the classic culprits are mud daubers (those little wasps love to build nests in the tube), spider webs, and even a stray leaf or a bit of shop rag left over from maintenance. Even a tiny speck of dirt can partially block the opening, which makes the reading lag or read low—especially dangerous on takeoff when you need accurate speed. And don’t forget the static port: if that gets blocked by a bug or a paint chip, the pressure difference goes haywire, and the airspeed can read high or low depending on altitude. In a Cessna, the pitot tube is right out in the open, so a preflight check is your only real defense—you literally look into the hole and make sure it’s clear. Some pilots even carry a small pipe cleaner to poke it out, just in case.",
